====Noun====
{{en-noun|~|s|staves|*}}
[[Image:Music-staff.png|thumb|right|A musical staff|class=skin-invert-image]]

# {{lb|en|plural {{m|en|staffs}} or {{m|en|staves}}}} A long, straight, thick wooden [[rod]] or [[stick]], especially one used to assist in walking.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Exodus|12|11|page=147|column=2|passage=And thus ſhall ye eate it [the lamb]: with your loines girded, your ſhooes on your feet, and your '''ſtaffe''' in your hand: and ye ſhall eate it in haſte: it is the {{smc|Lords}} Paſſeouer.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1927|author=[http://openlibrary.org/authors/OL2416183A F. E. Penny]
|chapter=4|title=Pulling the Strings
|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL16814587W
|passage=The case was that of a murder. It had an element of mystery about it, however, which was puzzling the authorities. A turban and loincloth soaked in blood had been found; also a '''staff'''.}}
# {{lb|en|music|plural {{m|en|staffs}} or {{m|en|staves}}}} A [[series]] of [[horizontal]] [[line]]s on which [[music]]al [[note]]s are written; a [[stave]].
# {{lb|en|plural {{m|en|staff}} or {{m|en|staffs}}}} People [[employed]] by a [[business]] or other [[organisation]].
#: {{ux|en|The company employed 10 new [[member of staff|members of '''staff''']] this month.}}
#: {{ux|en|The company has taken on 1600 more highly-paid '''staff'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1940|month=July|title=Notes and News: A Magnificent Transport Achievement|journal=Railway Magazine|page=419|text=No department of the Southern Railway escaped some share of the work involved, and the outdoor traffic and locomotive '''staffs''' in particular were engaged literally night and day, snatching a few hours' sleep as opportunity offered, until the task was completed.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=December 16, 2011|author=Denis Campbell
|title=Hospital staff 'lack skills to cope with dementia patients'
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/society/2011/dec/16/hospital-staff-dementia-patients
|passage=Most '''staff''' do not have the skills to cope with such challenging patients, who too often receive "impersonal" care and suffer from boredom, the first National Audit of Dementia found. It says hospitals should introduce "dementia champions".}}
#* {{RQ:FT|edition=weekend|author=Rebecca Rose|date=2023-11-25|title=How old is too old for a profile pic?|section=Life & Arts|page=22|passage=It turns out that, in journalistic terms, and especially at the FT, where many '''staff''' see out their entire careers, seven years is nothing.}}
# {{lb|en|construction|uncountable}} A mixture of [[plaster]] and [[fibre]] used as a temporary exterior wall covering.
# A pole, stick, or wand borne as an ensign of authority; a badge of office.
#: {{ux|en|a constable's '''staff'''}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|I|ii|122|2|passage=Me thought this '''ſtaffe''' mine Office-badge in Court / Was broke in twaine:}}
#* {{RQ:Hayward Edward 6|passage=All his officers brake their '''staves'''; but at their return new '''staves''' were delivered unto them.}}
# A pole upon which a [[flag]] is supported and displayed.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} The [[rung]] of a [[ladder]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1739|author={{w|John Campbell|John Campbell}}|title=The Travels and Adventures of Edward Bevan, Esq.
|passage=I ascend at one [ladder] of six hundred and thirty-nine '''staves'''.}}
# A series of verses so disposed that, when it is concluded, the same order begins again; a stanza; a [[stave]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|To the Most Honourable ''John'', Lord Marquess of ''Normanby'', Earl of ''Mulgrave'', &c. and Knight of the ''Most Noble Order of the Garter''|page=192|passage=Mr. ''Cowley'' had found out, that no kind of '''Staff''' is proper for an Heroick Poem; as being all too lirical:}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author=James George Frazer|title=Folk-Lore In The Old Testament|volume=1|page=20|text=At this point the reciter of the story chants the following '''staves''':—<br>"''Oh! the Day-horse. Oh! the Day-horse,<br>The Day-horse has gone to the river Gang,<br>The Day-horse has floated to the Sora Sea,<br>Oh! the Day-horse.''"}}
# {{lb|en|engineering}} An [[arbor]], as of a wheel or a pinion of a watch.
# {{lb|en|surgery}} The grooved director for the gorget, or knife, used in cutting for stone in the bladder.
# {{lb|en|military}} An establishment of officers in various departments attached to an army, to a section of an army, or to the commander of an army. The general's staff consists of those officers about his person who are employed in carrying his commands into execution.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Barnaby Rudge|volume=III|49|217|At the head of that division which had Westminster Bridge for its approach to the scene of action, Lord George Gordon took his post; with Gashford at his right hand, and sundry ruffians, of most unpromising appearance, forming a kind of '''staff''' about him.}}
# {{lb|en|rail|archaic}} A form of token once used, in combination with a ticket, for safe train movements between two points on a single line.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1946|month=July and August|title=Wise's Train '''Staff'''|journal=Railway Magazine|page=214|text=The train-'''staff''' and ticket system was used widely at one time, until superseded by electrical token systems, the first of which, the tablet system, appeared in 1878, {{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|month=November and December|author=K. Longbottom|title=By Goods Train to Gweedore|journal=Railway Magazine|page=355|text=The first up train was the morning semi-fast ''ex'' Buncrana, which sped through with No. 8 at its head, adroitly exchanging '''staffs''' at about 15 m.p.h. {{...}} The next train through Tooban was our opposite number, and we duly received the vital '''staff'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=November|author=R. K. Kirkland|title=The Wimbledon and West Croydon Line of the Southern Region|journal=Railway Magazine|page=726|text=The unusual rolling stock, the fare collection methods, and the exchange of train '''staffs''' make it quite clear that here is something out of the ordinary run of suburban electric lines.}}
